['Creating a service as described with all the points outlined would be beyond the scope of this platform as it requires a lot of work and time to develop fully functional code. However, I can provide you with some sample pseudo-code demonstrating how to approach certain parts of your project. 

1. Integrating with third-party REST API:
```java
@Service
public class PokemonService {
    private final RestTemplate restTemplate;
    private final String apiUrl = "https://pokeapi.co/api/v2/pokemon/{name}"; // example API URL

    public PokemonService(RestTemplateBuilder builder) {
        this.restTemplate = builder.build();
    }

    @Retryable(value = Exception.class, maxAttempts = 3, backoff = @Backoff(delay = 500))
    @CircuitBreaker(name = "pokemonService", fallbackMethod = "getDefaultPokemon")
    public Pokemon getPokemonByName(String name) {
        return restTemplate.getForObject(apiUrl, Pokemon.class, name);
    }
}
```
2. Concurrency and Performance:
```java
@Service
public class AsyncPokemonService {
    private final Executor executor;
    private final PokemonService pokemonService;

    public AsyncPokemonService(Executor executor, PokemonService pokemonService) {
        this.executor = executor;
        this.pokemonService = pokemonService;
    }

    @Async("threadPoolTaskExecutor") // Assuming you have a custom Thread Pool configured.
    public CompletableFuture<Pokemon> getPokemonByName(String name) {
        return CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> pokemonService.getPokemonByName(name), executor);
    }
}
```
3. Error Handling: 

This is handled via the @Retryable and @CircuitBreaker annotations in our service. We set a maximum number of attempts for retries (e.g., three times) and specify a delay between each attempt (half a second).
4. Testing:
```java
