["Here's a basic skeleton of what your Node.js application might look like using some popular third-party libraries:

```javascript
const fs = require('fs');
const readline = require('readline');
const { Worker } = require('worker_threads');
const EventEmitter = require('events');

class DataProcessingPipeline extends EventEmitter {
    constructor() {
        super();

        // Configuration with default values
        this.bufferSize = 1024;
        this.errorTolerance = 5;
        this.loggingLevel = 'info';

        // Initialize error count
        this.errorCount = 0;
    }

    configure({ bufferSize, errorTolerance, loggingLevel }) {
        if (bufferSize) this.bufferSize = bufferSize;
        if (errorTolerance) this.errorTolerance = errorTolerance;
        if (loggingLevel) this.loggingLevel = loggingLevel;
    }

    processFile(filename, workerScriptPath) {
        const workerThreads = [];

        // Create a read stream and set encoding to binary
        const inputStream = fs.createReadStream(filename, { encoding: 'binary' });

        // Create an instance of the worker thread per each bufferSize
        for (let i = 0; i < this.bufferSize; i++) {
            const workerThread = new Worker(workerScriptPath);

            workerThreads.push(workerThread);

            // Handle messages from worker threads
            workerThread.on('message', (result) => {
                this.emit('data', result);
            });

            // Handle errors
            workerThread.on('error', (err) => {
                console.log(`Worker thread error: ${err}`);
                if (++this.errorCount >= this.errorTolerance) {
                    console.log(`Error tolerance threshold reached, terminating...`);
                    process.exit(1); // Adjust according to error handling strategy
                }
            });
        }

        let index = 0;

        const rl = readline.createInterface({ input: inputStream });

        rl.on('line', (line) => {
            const bufferIndex = index % this.bufferSize;

            // Send a new chunk to the corresponding worker thread for processing
            workerThreads[bufferIndex].postMessage(line);
            index++;
        });

        rl.on('close', () => {
            // Terminate all worker threads when input stream is closed
            workerThreads.forEach((workerThread) => workerThread.terminate());

            console.log('Processing completed');
            this.emit('end');
        });
    }
}

module.exports = DataProcessingPipeline;
```

This is a simplified skeleton of the pipeline which uses worker threads for parallel processing and event-driven architecture to handle streaming data from sources. The `processFile` function shows how you might integrate with an external library (`readline`) to stream data in chunks.

Please note that this is a basic implementation and requires additional work including configuration setup, error handling, backpressure mechanisms, unit tests for worker threads etc. Also, ensure to handle edge cases such as empty files or directories when processing multiple files at once. For actual implementation, you might need to install dependencies like `readline` and `worker_threads` using npm:
```bash
npm install readline worker_threads
``` 

This basic pipeline could be extended further with more functionalities as per your requirements. The error handling mechanism can be adjusted depending on the severity of errors you want to handle, such as retries or ignoring certain types of errors. Also remember that in a real scenario you would need to write your worker script which will process each chunk of data received from main thread and emit an event with processed data.
"]

["This task is quite complex, and a complete solution would require significant time and effort to develop an entire library with all of these features, so here's just a simplified code snippet for your understanding. 

For network error handling, we can use 'axios'. For caching, we could consider using 'lru-cache'. Pagination requires server support. Simultaneous requests are managed by JavaScript promises. Modularity and maintainability come from clear module design and good test coverage. Adaptation to evolving API endpoints is ensured through dynamic imports and function parameters.

```javascript
// External libraries
const axios = require('axios');
const LRU = require('lru-cache');

class DomTraversal {
  constructor(apiList, cacheSize = 10) {
    this.apis = apiList;
    this.cache = new LRU({ max: cacheSize });
  }

  traverseDOM(domTree, criterion) {
    let results = [];

    function processNode(node) {
      const metadataPromises = this.apis.map((api) => axios.get(api + node));

      Promise.all(metadataPromises)
        .then((responses) => {
          responses.forEach((response, index) => {
            if (criterion(response.data)) {
              results.push({ node: node, metadata: response.data });
            }

            const children = Array.from(node.children);
            children.forEach((child) => processNode(child));
          })
        .catch((error) => console.log('Network error', error)) // Handle network errors gracefully
      );
    }

    processNode(domTree);

    return results;
  }
}

module.exports = DomTraversal;
```
Note: This is a simplified version of the requested library and many aspects are not covered in detail, such as pagination handling or caching strategy. A complete library would require more sophisticated network error handling and caching strategies, proper unit testing, extensive documentation and possibly a build process for easy updates and integration into various projects. 

