Below is an outline of how you could structure your C-based background process manager. For simplicity, this code assumes some functionalities are not present (like logging, alerting, external system integrations etc.). In real implementation these would be much more complex tasks that require additional libraries and services:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdatomic.h>

typedef struct {
    int id;
    // other fields for command details
} Command;

_Atomic(int) concurrentCommands = 0;
int maxConcurrentCommands;  // configurable via function call or file config
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
pthread_cond_t cond  = PTHREAD_COND_INITIALIZER;

void *executeCommand(void *arg) {
    Command *cmd = (Command *) arg;

    // your command execution code here
    printf("Executing command %d\n", cmd->id);

    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    concurrentCommands--;
    pthread_cond_signal(&cond);  // signal to shutdown thread if we are under limit
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);

    return NULL;
}

void addCommand(Command *cmd) {
    pthread_t tid;
    pthread_attr_t attr;

    // Wait until there's room for a new command
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    while (concurrentCommands >= maxConcurrentCommands) {
        printf("Limit exceeded, waiting...\n");
        pthread_cond_wait(&cond, &lock);  // unlock and wait until we have room
    }

    concurrentCommands++;
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);

    // Start a new thread to execute the command
    pthread_attr_init(&attr);
    pthread_create(&tid, &attr, executeCommand, cmd);
}
```
This code snippet only covers requirement #1 (support asynchronous execution of customizable commands). To fully satisfy all requirements you would need to expand this into a full-fledged service with robust error handling, external system integration, and extensive unit/integration testing. Given the complexity of the task, it may be best implemented in a language like C++ or Python that has more complete support for concurrent programming and networking, as well as libraries for logging, monitoring, and testing.
