["Here is a basic implementation of a `StateManager` class in Java that uses AWS S3 bucket for file storage service. It's not complete and should be expanded to meet all requirements but provides a starting point:

```java
import com.amazonaws.services.s3.AmazonS3;
import com.amazonaws.services.s3.model.GetObjectRequest;
import com.amazonaws.services.s3.model.PutObjectRequest;
import java.io.*;

public class StateManager {
    private AmazonS3 s3Client;
    private String bucketName;
    private String key;

    public StateManager(AmazonS3 s3Client, String bucketName, String key) {
        this.s3Client = s3Client;
        this.bucketName = bucketName;
        this.key = key;
    }

    // Load the state object from S3
    public Object loadState() throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException {
        GetObjectRequest rangeObjectRequest = new GetObjectRequest(bucketName, key);
        try (S3Object s3Object = s3Client.getObject(rangeObjectRequest)) {
            try (S3ObjectInputStream objectContent = s3Object.getObjectContent()) {
                ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(objectContent);
                return ois.readUnshared();
            }
        }
    }

    // Save the state object to S3
    public void saveState(Serializable obj) throws IOException {
        try (ByteArrayOutputStream baos = new ByteArrayOutputStream()) {
            try (ObjectOutputStream oos = new ObjectOutputStream(baos)) {
                oos.writeUnshared(obj);
                byte[] bytes = baos.toByteArray();
                InputStream stream = new ByteArrayInputStream(bytes);
                s3Client.putObject(new PutObjectRequest(bucketName, key, stream, null));
            }
        }
    }
}
```
This class uses the AWS SDK for Java to interact with S3. The `loadState` and `saveState` methods handle serialization and deserialization of the state object. Please note that this is a simplified version and does not cover all the requirements in your question. For example, it doesn't implement any kind of locking or caching strategies for handling concurrent access, updates, and storage migrations. You would need to expand upon this concept to meet all the requirements you have outlined.
