['Here is a simplified code representation of how you might design this `StateManager` class in Java. It does not cover the whole complexity as it would require implementing multiple systems like Configuration Management System, Monitoring Service, Distributed Tracing etc. The code below only serves as an example and can be expanded upon with more detailed implementation details:

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantReadWriteLock;

public class StateManager {
    private final ReentrantReadWriteLock lock = new ReentrantReadWriteLock();
    private final File file = getFileFromConfigurations(); // replace this with your configuration logic

    public Object readState() throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException {
        lock.readLock().lock();
        try (ObjectInputStream in = new ObjectInputStream(new BufferedInputStream(new FileInputStream(file)))) {
            return in.readUnshared();
        } finally {
            lock.readLock().unlock();
        }
    }

    public void writeState(Object state) throws IOException {
        lock.writeLock().lock();
        try (ObjectOutputStream out = new ObjectOutputStream(new BufferedOutputStream(new FileOutputStream(file)))) {
            out.writeUnshared(state);
        } finally {
            lock.writeLock().unlock();
        }
    }

    private File getFileFromConfigurations() {
        // This method should implement the logic to read configurations from multiple sources (environment variables, local file etc)
        return new File("state.data"); 
    }
}
```

This class uses `ReentrantReadWriteLock` to ensure thread safety and protect against concurrent modifications. It reads and writes the state object by serializing/deserializing it using Java's built-in Object I/O Stream classes. 

Please note that this is a simplified version, production grade code would need additional error handling, logging, configuration management systems, distributed tracing etc which are not covered in this example. This is also assuming you have the file path and other configurations available to instantiate the StateManager class. In real world applications these dependencies should be injected instead of hard-coded or retrieved at runtime.
