['This task is quite complex and involves various aspects such as networking, multithreading, database interaction, error handling, logging, performance optimization, unit testing, and more. Here's a simplified example to give you an idea of how it might start. This isn't a complete solution but should give you an idea on how to approach the problem.

```C
#include <stdio.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <unistd.h>  // for sleep()

// Assume we have some complex struct or class representing transactions
typedef struct Transaction {
    int age;
    int recent_transaction_count;
} Transaction;

// Global mutex variable to protect shared data across threads
pthread_mutex_t lock; 

void* processTransaction(void *args) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    // Cast back to the known type and use it
    Transaction *transaction = (Transaction*) args;
    printf("Processing transaction: Age: %d, Recent Txn count: %d\n", 
            transaction->age, transaction->recent_transaction_count);

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

int main() {
    if (pthread_mutex_init(&lock, NULL) != 0) {
        printf("\n mutex init failed\n");
        return 1;
    }

    // Assume we have a list of Transactions to process
    Transaction transactions[5] = {{20, 3}, {30, 7}, {40, 9}, {50, 10}, {60, 15}};

    pthread_t threads[5];

    for (int i = 0; i < 5; ++i) {
        int rc = pthread_create(&threads[i], NULL, processTransaction, &transactions[i]);

        if (rc != 0) {
            printf("Error: Unable to create thread, %d\n", rc);
            return -1;
        }
    }

    // Join all threads 
    for(int i = 0; i < 5; ++i) {
        pthread_join(threads[i], NULL);        
    }

    printf("All transactions processed\n");
    return 0;
}
```
The above code demonstrates thread-safe processing of transactions using POSIX threads. The actual solution would be much more complex and involve networking, database interaction, error handling, logging, performance optimization, unit testing, etc.
