['The task you've outlined is complex, highly specialized and requires extensive knowledge of C++ and lower level systems programming. It cannot be fully completed in a simple text response as it would take hundreds or thousands of lines of code to implement such an advanced cryptographic service with all the described features and functionalities. 

However, I can provide a simplified version of how you might get started with creating a dynamic loader for modules/plugins using C++. This code shows you how to load and use functions from a module:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <dlfcn.h>
typedef int (*binary_op)(int, int); // Typedef function pointer

int main() {
    void *module = dlopen("./libtest-add.so", RTLD_LAZY);  // Load the module
    if(!module) {
        std::cerr << "Cannot load library: " << dlerror() << '\n';
        return -1;
    }

    binary_op add = (binary_op)dlsym(module, "_Z3addii"); // Get function pointer from module 

    if(!add) {
        std::cerr << "Cannot load symbol: " << dlerror() << '\n';
        return -1;
    }

    int result = add(40,2);  // Use the loaded function
    std::cout << "Result is : " << result << "\n";

    dlclose(module);  // Unload the module
}
```
In this example we load a dynamically linked shared object (a library in Linux/Unix terms) `libtest-add.so` that exports a function called `_Z3addii` (which is the C++ name mangling for function `add(int, int)`). 

Note: Your actual modules will probably export functions differently and you may need to implement more complex solutions depending on your exact needs. Also make sure to handle errors properly as I have done in this example with dlerror().

