['This is a quite complex programming task and it cannot be fully covered in one code block due to its complexity, but I can provide you with a simplified example of how to implement some key aspects such as selective deserialization and error handling using C#. 

Please note that this is not a complete solution, but it could serve as a starting point for your library development. 

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using Newtonsoft.Json; // You need to install this package from NuGet Package Manager
using Newtonsoft.Json.Linq;

public class CustomDeserializer
{
    private JsonSerializer _serializer;
    private bool _deserializeAllProperties = true;
    private string[] _propertiesToIgnore;

    public CustomDeserializer(string[] propertiesToIgnore)
    {
        _propertiesToIgnore = propertiesToIgnore;
        _serializer = new JsonSerializer()
        {
            MissingMemberHandling = MissingMemberHandling.Ignore,
        };
    }

    // Set this to true if you want to deserialize all properties
    public void DeserializeAllProperties(bool deserializeAll)
    {
        _deserializeAllProperties = deserializeAll;
    }

    public T Deserialize<T>(string jsonString) where T : new()
    {
        using (var stringReader = new StringReader(jsonString))
        using (var jsonReader = new JsonTextReader(stringReader))
        {
            try
            {
                if (_deserializeAllProperties || _propertiesToIgnore != null && _propertiesToIgnore.Length > 0)
                {
                    var token = JToken.Load(jsonReader);

                    // Iterate through all tokens and remove ignored properties
                    RemoveIgnoredOrEmptyTokens(token);

                    jsonString = JsonConvert.SerializeObject(token, Formatting.None);
                }
            }
            catch (JsonReaderException)
            {
                throw; // Handle error here
            }
        }

        try
        {
            return JsonConvert.DeserializeObject<T>(jsonString);
        }
        catch (JsonSerializationException ex)
        {
            throw new Exception("Error deserializing the object", ex);
        }
    }

    private void RemoveIgnoredOrEmptyTokens(JToken token, string path = "")
    {
        // If this is an object then remove properties to ignore
        if (token.Type == JTokenType.Object)
        {
            var copy = token;

            foreach (var prop in _propertiesToIgnore ?? new string[0])
            {
                var childToken = token.SelectToken(path + prop);
                if (childToken != null)
                    childToken.Remove();
            }
        }
    }
}
```
This code provides a `CustomDeserializer` class which allows users to selectively deserialize properties from JSON strings and ignore specified ones or all of them by setting the appropriate flag. It uses the Newtonsoft Json library for parsing, manipulating, and serializing JSON objects in C#. 

Please note that implementing the entire solution you described involves many more aspects including error handling, concurrent access management, performance optimization, selective deserialization based on user-defined criteria, integration with multiple external REST APIs for metadata verification, version control, and dependency resolution etc. This code is meant to be a starting point and would need to be expanded upon significantly to fulfill your requirements.
']

