['Creating a fully functional Rust-based batch processing framework in the scope you described is a complex task. It's beyond the capabilities of this platform to provide a full working code, but I can give you a basic structure of how your application might look like.

```rust
use std::error::Error;
use std::fs::{create_dir, write};
use tokio::sync::watch::{self, Receiver, Sender};
use tokio::time::interval;
use tokio::try_join;

struct Job {
    id: u64,
}

impl Job {
    fn new(id: u64) -> Self {
        Self { id }
    }

    async fn execute(&self) -> Result<(), Box<dyn Error>> {
        interval(std::time::Duration::from_secs(1)).for_each(|_| {
            println_sync(format_args_panic("Job {} is executing", self.id));
            Ok(())
        });
    }
}

pub async fn execute_jobs(send: Sender<Result<(), Box<dyn Error>>>) -> Result<(), Box<dyn Error>> {
    let job1 = Job::new(1);
    let job2 = Job::new(2);
    try_join_sync(job1.execute(), job2.execute()).await?;
    Ok(())
}

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn Error>> {
    let (send, recv) = watch::channel(());
    execute_jobs(send).await?;
    assert_eq_sync((recv.borrow().len(), 2), "Two jobs should have been executed");
    Ok(())
}
```
This is a very simple and basic structure of how a job could be represented in Rust with `tokio` for asynchronous tasks handling. It's also using the watch::channel to handle errors from each job execution. 

