['This task involves developing a complex cryptographic service in C++ using OpenSSL library for AES encryption/decryption. Writing code for such an extensive task is beyond the scope of this platform but I can provide you with a simplified example of how to initialize and clean up an AES-256-CBC cipher using OpenSSL. 

Please note that this code requires you have OpenSSL installed and properly linked in your project. Also, remember to handle errors as needed according to your system requirements.

```c++
#include <openssl/evp.h>
#include <string.h>

EVP_CIPHER_CTX* init_aes256cbc(const char *password) {
    EVP_CIPHER_CTX *ctx;
    unsigned char key[32]; // 256-bit (32 bytes) AES-256 key
    unsigned char iv[16]; // 128-bit IV

    /* Derive key using SHA-1 */
    EVP_BytesToKey(EVP_aes_256_cbc(), EVP_sha1(), NULL, password, strlen(password), 1, key, iv);

    ctx = EVP_CIPHER_CTX_new(); // Create new cipher context

    /* Initialize the encryption operation using AES-256-CBC. */
    if(!EVP_EncryptInit_ex(ctx, EVP_aes_256_cbc(), NULL, key, iv)) {
        return nullptr; // Handle error here as needed.
    }

    /* Set the padding to PKCS7 for block ciphers */
    EVP_CIPHER_CTX_set_padding(ctx, 1);

    return ctx; // Return initialized context
}

void cleanup_aes256cbc(EVP_CIPHER_CTX *ctx) {
    /* Clean up and disable the cipher */
    EVP_CIPHER_CTX_free(ctx); 
}
```

The `init_aes256cbc` function initializes an AES-256-CBC cipher context using OpenSSL library. It takes a password as input and derives the key and IV from it using SHA-1 hash. The function also sets padding to PKCS7 for block ciphers.
The `cleanup_aes256cbc` function cleans up and disables an AES-256-CBC cipher context, freeing the associated resources.

Please note that this is a basic example and does not cover all aspects of your task such as dynamic loading of external libraries, handling version mismatches, managing keys in a high-traffic database etc. You would need to implement these features based on your specific needs using additional OpenSSL functions, error checking, memory management, thread safety considerations and more.
