["Creating a full framework with all these features will be quite lengthy, but here is a simplified outline of how you might approach this task using modern JavaScript principles like Promises and async/await for handling asynchronous operations, Proxy for creating caching mechanism, and Webpack or Rollup for module bundling.

```javascript
class RestfulAPI {
  constructor(baseURL) {
    this.baseURL = baseURL;
    this.cache = new Map();
    this.pendingRequests = new Map();
  }

  get(endpoint, params = {}, options = {}) {
    const cacheKey = JSON.stringify({ endpoint, params });

    if (this.cache.has(cacheKey)) return Promise.resolve(this.cache.get(cacheKey));
    if (this.pendingRequests.has(cacheKey)) return this.pendingRequests.get(cacheKey);

    const promise = fetch(`${this.baseURL}/${endpoint}?${new URLSearchParams(params)}`, options)
      .then(response => response.json())
      .catch(error => { throw new Error('Network error: ' + error); });

    this.pendingRequests.set(cacheKey, promise);

    return promise.then(result => {
      this.cache.set(cacheKey, result);
      this.pendingRequests.delete(cacheKey);

      return result;
    });
  }
}

class App {
  constructor() {
    this.api = new RestfulAPI('https://example.com/api');
    this.modules = [];
  }

  async loadModule(moduleName) {
    const modulePath = `./modules/${moduleName}`;

    if (!this.modules[moduleName]) {
      try {
        const module = await import(modulePath);

        this.modules[moduleName] = module;
        console.log('Module loaded:', moduleName);
      } catch (error) {
        console.error('Error loading module:', error);
      }
    } else {
      console.warn('Module already loaded:', moduleName);
    }
  }

  async executeAPI(endpoint, params = {}, options = {}) {
    return this.api.get(endpoint, params, options)
      .then(result => {
        // Handle the result here or pass it to modules for further processing
        console.log('API Result:', result);

        this.modules.forEach(module => module.handleResult && module.handleResult(result));

        return result;
      })
      .catch(error => { throw new Error(`Failed to execute API: ${error}`) });
  }
}
```
This is a simplified example, but it provides a starting point for your framework. In real-world scenarios, you'd want to add more features such as pagination, error handling, caching mechanism, and module management. Also, make sure to use appropriate HTTP methods (GET/POST etc.) based on the API requirements.
"]

