['Here's a simple implementation of the `updatePassword` function in PHP with some parts mentioned in your requirements. Note that, it does not cover all technical aspects you asked for due to complexity and size constraints. 

```php
<?php
// Include necessary libraries/dependencies
require_once 'vendor/autoload.php';
use \Firebase\JWT\JWT;

class User {
    private $db; // Assume a PDO instance is defined here for database operations

    public function __construct($pdo) {
        $this->db = $pdo;
    }

    public function updatePassword($jwt, $oldPass, $newPass) {
        // Validate new password complexity
        if (!$this->validateComplexity($newPass)) {
            throw new Exception("Password does not meet complexity requirements.");
        }

        try {
            $decoded = JWT::decode($jwt, $_ENV['JWT_SECRET'], array('HS256')); // Decode the token from environment variable

            if (password_verify($oldPass, $this->getUserPasswordHash($decoded->sub))) { 
                // If old password matches
                $newHashed = password_hash($newPass, PASSWORD_DEFAULT);

                $stmt = $this->db->prepare("UPDATE users SET password=? WHERE id=?");
                $stmt->execute([$newHashed, $decoded->sub]); // Update the new hashed password in the database
            } else {
                throw new Exception('Old password does not match.');
            }
        } catch (Exception $e) {
            error_log($e->getMessage());
            throw $e;
        }
    }

    private function validateComplexity($pass) { 
        // Check the length, including at least one uppercase letter, lowercase letter, number and special character.
        $regex = "/^(?=.*[a-z])(?=.*[A-Z])(?=.*\d)(?=.*(_|[^\w])).+$/";
        return preg_match($regex, $pass) === 1 && strlen($pass) >= 12;
    }

    private function getUserPasswordHash($userId) { 
        $stmt = $this->db->prepare("SELECT password FROM users WHERE id=?");
        $stmt->execute([$userId]); // Fetch user's hashed password from the database
        return $stmt->fetchColumn();
    }
}
?>
```
This PHP code uses JSON Web Tokens (JWT) for session management, password hashing with bcrypt to ensure security, and JWT library for handling token decoding. The code also includes a basic complexity check for the new password and an example of how you might store user credentials in a database using prepared statements and parameterized queries to prevent SQL injection attacks.
']

