    public String greet() {
        return "Real-time data processing...";
    }
}
```

This is a basic example of a Spring Cloud Stream application, which you can use to process real time data from diverse sources. 

For the integration with APIs like Bloomberg, Reuters, Yahoo Finance etc., you'd need to create separate services for each one that call their respective API and push the data into the message broker (RabbitMQ or Kafka).

For caching layer, Redis is a good option. You can use Spring's @Cacheable annotation on methods which fetch data from sources. For eviction policy, you can implement your own logic based on data patterns and source reliability.

As for resource management, you might want to look at Kubernetes' auto-scaling feature or custom written load balancing algorithms.

Monitoring and alerting can be achieved using Spring Boot Actuator which provides several endpoints like /metrics, /health etc. You can also use Prometheus along with Grafana for more advanced monitoring and alerting. 

Error handling and data consistency is a broad topic in itself and involves concepts like Saga pattern or Two-phase commit. These are complex topics that need careful design and implementation.

The documentation will be generated using tools such as Swagger or Spring REST Docs, depending on the complexity of your service and its interactions with other services.

This is just a high level view and it can get much more complex based on actual requirements like data processing pipelines, anomaly detection algorithms, cache eviction policies, resource management strategies etc. Each of these topics deserve separate detailed discussions and implementation plans.
